  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1", "agent workload and its log groups (discover)", "NAT gateways / VPC egress path / proxies / DNS resolver (discover)", "Secrets Manager / SSM params holding the OpenAI API key (check only metadata/rotation/change events, never read secret values)"],
  "timeframe": "Incident window: 2026-11-25T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-25T08:54:00Z (narrow down onset time precisely). Baseline: same hours on 2026-11-24 and the prior 24h before onset.",
  "criteria": "1) Characterize the failures: onset time, rate over time (step change vs gradual vs intermittent), and error breakdown from app logs: HTTP status codes (429 rate limit / quota, 401/403 auth, 5xx e.g. 500/502/503/529 from provider, 400 invalid request), timeouts (connect vs read), DNS failures, TLS/connection reset errors. Provider-side signatures: 5xx/overloaded errors with OpenAI response bodies or x-request-id headers, errors uniformly distributed across hosts/AZs/NATs, onset not aligned with any change on our side. Customer-side signatures: 429 insufficient_quota/rate limit tied to account/tier or our own concurrency increase, 401 after key rotation, connect timeouts/resets correlating with a specific NAT gateway/AZ/subnet, NAT ErrorPortAllocation / PacketsDropCount / connection-count saturation, DNS resolution failures, security group/NACL/route table changes, VPC endpoint/proxy changes, Lambda/ECS/EKS config or concurrency changes, new deployments or dependency/SDK version changes, retry-storm patterns. 2) Check whether failures cluster by AZ, host, task, pod, or NAT gateway (all-uniform suggests provider; clustered suggests us). 3) Check CloudWatch metrics for NAT gateways, load balancers, Lambda/ECS/EKS as applicable, and X-Ray traces if present for outbound call spans to api.openai.com. 4) Use CloudTrail (via the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill) to list infra changes (networking, IAM, secrets, deployments, scaling) in the 48h before onset and judge whether any plausibly explains the failures. 5) Check deployments/commits landed before onset. 6) Note explicitly why the customer's own metrics look normal (e.g. retries masking failures, metrics not instrumented on the outbound call, errors caught and returned as fallbacks). Report: verdict (provider / us / undetermined) with confidence, key evidence with timestamps and counts, what you could not check, and 2-3 concrete next steps. Read-only only; no mutating AWS calls."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI call failures: logs, network egress, and recent changes"}], "created_at": null}

